/target
**/*.rs.bk
Cargo.lock

# Benchmark outputs. The shared *inputs* under benches/data/ ARE tracked, so
# that a comparison run is reproducible; the results are machine-specific.
/benches/results/

# Fase 6's competitive benchmark suite is an application (a fixed set of
# pinned third-party competitor versions), not a published library, so its
# Cargo.lock IS committed -- the opposite convention from the main
# workspace's own Cargo.lock above, and deliberate: see
# benchmarks/competitive/README.md and Fase 6 Benchmark.md's own
# VERSION PINNING section ("Generar un lockfile reproducible").
!/benchmarks/competitive/Cargo.lock

# Rendered documentation. The VitePress *source* under site/ is tracked; the
# build output is produced by CI and published to GitHub Pages.
/site/.vitepress/dist/
/site/.vitepress/cache/
/site/node_modules/

# Generated by site/check-snippets.py: every published snippet, extracted
# so Cargo can compile and run it. Regenerate rather than commit.
/crates/verbora-examples/examples/doc_snippets.rs

# Third-party model/dictionary assets the POS-tagging and spellcheck
# competitive benches need (a pretrained perceptron model, a MobileBERT
# checkpoint, a Hunspell .aff/.dic pair) -- separately licensed, tens of MB,
# fetched on demand by benchmarks/competitive/scripts/fetch-models.sh, never
# vendored. Same reasoning as the WordNet database (see
# crates/verbora-wordnet's crate docs): a missing licence-restricted asset
# should not fail a build, so every bench that reads from here skips cleanly
# with a printed notice when it is absent.
/benchmarks/competitive/models/

# graft's local graph cache — regenerable, not committed (run `graft build`).
graft/
